WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.460
Inter-process communication is a trust boundary.

2
00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:09.880
A privileged helper cannot assume a request is safe simply because it arrived through the expected channel.

3
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:20.620
Messages need clear schemas, versioning, length limits, type checks, caller identity where available, and authorization rules that match the operation being requested.

4
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:27.620
The privileged side should validate all inputs independently, even when the low-privilege client already performed validation.

5
00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:33.720
Client-side validation improves user experience, but boundary-side validation protects the authority.

6
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:43.260
The helper should check command names, allowed values, paths, handles, sizes, operation modes, and state transitions before acting.

7
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.420
Authorization belongs where the authority exists.

8
00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:53.240
The broker or helper should decide whether the caller may perform the requested action on the requested target at the requested time.

9
00:00:53.340 --> 00:01:02.860
That decision may depend on platform identity, session context, user approval, policy settings, resource ownership, or deployment configuration.

10
00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:06.020
Error responses should be safe and useful.

11
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:18.760
They can tell the client that a request was denied, malformed, unsupported, or temporarily unavailable without exposing secrets, internal paths, privileged state, or unnecessary implementation details.

12
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:23.340
Consistent errors also help clients handle failure without guessing.

13
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:27.380
IPC contracts should be treated as stable interfaces.

14
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:39.000
Adding a new command, field, or version changes the privileged surface, so it deserves design review, compatibility tests, negative tests, and operational documentation.
